<section class="section wrap" id="research"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">01 / RESEARCH</span><span class="mini">THE QUESTION BEHIND THE WORK</span></div><div class="research-grid"><h2>A connected network<br>isn’t always an<br><em>accessible one.</em></h2><div><p class="large">How can freight disadvantage be measured—and reduced—through engineering analysis?</p><p class="muted">My doctoral research at Warsaw University of Technology investigates transport poverty in freight transportation networks: how infrastructure limitations, service gaps and operational constraints affect economic connectivity.</p><p class="muted">I combine accessibility analysis with transport modelling to develop and test ways of describing those disadvantages. The framework is evolving; empirical validation is part of the work ahead.</p><div class="supervisor">Doctoral research · Faculty of Transport<br>Supervisor: Prof. Mariusz Kostrzewski</div></div></div>
<div class="research-cards"><article><span class="card-number">A /</span><h3>Access & disadvantage</h3><p>Investigating where network connectivity, logistics services and economic barriers limit freight access.</p><span class="tag">ACCESSIBILITY INDICATORS</span></article><article><span class="card-number">B /</span><h3>Movement & interaction</h3><p>Using microscopic simulation to examine how passenger demand affects freight delay and reliability.</p><span class="tag">PTV VISSIM · CORRIDOR ANALYSIS</span></article><article><span class="card-number">C /</span><h3>Infrastructure & decisions</h3><p>Exploring how pavement condition and geometric constraints influence corridor performance and intervention priorities.</p><span class="tag">ENGINEERING ASSESSMENT</span></article></div>
</section>
